        confirmed unchanged. D2TG::Poller.pm is now 598 lines - still
        over the cap, entirely due to run_once itself; filed as
        follow-up TGT-276 for a full decomposition of that function.

2.17  2026-09-17
      - BUG FIX (TGT-273, found via a scheduled JOB-004 improvement
        hunt): run_once's edited_message branch had no redelivery-dedup
        guard at all, unlike the plain-message/media/voice branch which
        TGT-270 (this same session) hardened against Telegram's own
        at-least-once delivery. A redelivered edited_message update
        would print a duplicate NEW TG EDIT line every time. Naively
        reusing D2TG::Store::get_message the way TGT-270 did (checking
        mere row presence) does NOT work here - record_message UPSERTs
        on (chat_id, bot_key, message_id), so get_message already
        returns non-null for ANY message ever recorded, including the
        ORIGINAL pre-edit send; a presence check would wrongly suppress
        a genuinely new edit too. Fixed instead by comparing the
        incoming (sanitized) edited text against the already-stored
        summary: identical means this exact edit was already announced
        (a redelivery, skip); different or absent means a genuinely new
        edit or the first one (announce and record as before). Only
        applies when the edit carries text - a caption/media-only edit
        has no stored summary to compare against and is a documented,
        accepted gap. New t/273-edited-message-redelivery-skips-
        duplicate-announce.t covers redelivered-identical-edit (skip),
        genuinely-different-edit (still announces, summary updated),
        and first-ever-edit (no prior row, still announces).
      - MAINTENANCE: lib/D2TG/Poller.pm's own embedded POD (605 lines)
        extracted to lib/D2TG/Poller.pod while this ticket's fix
        touched the file, matching the Transcribe/Reply/Config
        precedent (REQ-028). Poller.pm is still 854 lines after that
        extraction, over this board's 500-line-per-module cap - filed
        as follow-up TGT-275 for a full decomposition rather than
        folding a large refactor into this bugfix (REQ-029).

