2.15  2026-09-16
      - BUGFIX (TGT-270, a live report from Michael via the budget
        project, filed as /tmp/ask-for-more-from-d2tg/20260916T194918-
        budget-oldmessage-replay.md): a poller version-change restart
        re-emitted a stale MEDIA DOWNLOAD ERROR that read exactly like
        a fresh live failure for a message that had actually already
        failed and been queued days earlier. Root cause:
        D2TG::Poller::run_once's redelivery-dedup guard only checked
        D2TG::Store::get_message (the messages table) before
        re-announcing a redelivered update - a media-download failure
        never calls record_message (only record_failed_download, a
        different table), so a genuine Telegram redelivery of that
        update_id (its own documented at-least-once delivery) was
        invisible to the guard and got legitimately re-processed as
        brand new. The same gap applied to voice/failed_transcriptions.
        record_failed_download's own ON CONFLICT upsert already makes
        the DATA layer idempotent for a redelivery - the gap was
        purely in the POLLER's own live announce/re-attempt path
        having no visibility into the retry queue before acting.
        Fixed by adding D2TG::Store::RetryQueue::has_failed_download/
        has_failed_transcription (forwarded through D2TG::Store) and
        extending run_once's redelivery-dedup guard to check both,
        alongside the existing messages-table check. New
        t/270-run-once-redelivery-skips-queued-failures.t (both
        download and transcription cases), confirmed genuinely red
        before the fix. Full test suite (202 files, 2216 tests) passes
        unchanged, 100% coverage on all 3 touched modules.

2.14  2026-09-16
      - REFACTOR (TGT-269, found via a scheduled JOB-004 improvement
        hunt): extract_bot_flag_or_die (D2TG::Reply::Args),
        extract_db_flag_or_die (D2TG::Config::Flags), and
        resolve_alias_dir_or_die/require_existing_base_dir_or_die
        (D2TG::Config::Paths) each independently hand-rolled the
        identical eval/print-STDERR/exit(1) wrapper idiom - the same
        duplication class TGT-172/230/236 already extracted (for
        callers wrapping ONE specific function each), just never
        noticed at the wrapper-generator level itself since each
        wrapper was added in a separate ticket. Extracted into new
        leaf module D2TG::OrDie (deliberately zero use-dependencies on
        D2TG::Config/D2TG::Config::Paths/D2TG::Reply, avoiding the
        circular-use trap: Config already uses Config::Paths, so
        Config::Paths cannot use Config back) with a single
        wantarray-aware or_die($coderef, @args) helper. All 4 wrappers
        reduced to one-line forwarders - zero behavior change, no
        caller-visible difference in exit code, STDERR text, or return
        shape. Full test suite (201 files, 2206 tests) passes
        unchanged, 100% coverage on all 4 touched/created modules. New
        t/269-or-die-shared-helper.t exercises the helper standalone.

2.13  2026-09-16
      - BUGFIX (TGT-268, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly bug
        hunt): TGT-264 fixed D2TG::Reply::Args::extract_bot_flag to
        die "--bot requires a value" for a sole bare --bot argument,
        instead of silently falling through - but cli/reply.pl and
        cli/send.pl each independently special-cased "if (@ARGV >= 2)
        { call extract_bot_flag_or_die } else { shift @ARGV }" around
        their own --bot branch, so when --bot was the ONLY remaining
        argument, both scripts took the else branch, silently
        discarded it, and fell through to a generic Usage error
        instead of TGT-264's own clear message - reintroducing the
        exact bug TGT-264 fixed, one layer up at these two callers.
        Removed the stale special-case in both scripts; they now call
        extract_bot_flag_or_die unconditionally, which already handles
