2.00  2026-09-14
      - BUGFIX (TGT-249, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly bug hunt):
        D2TG::Download::retry_failed_download and
        retry_failed_transcription's own $still_queued 3rd return value
        (TGT-244/TGT-248) was computed by unconditionally assuming the
        queue-row removal write (remove_failed_download /
        remove_failed_transcription) succeeded once record_message had
        succeeded - it never inspected D2TG::Poller::store_write_safe's
        own (ok, value) result for that call. When record_message
        succeeded but the removal write itself then hit a transient
        failure (a locked/busy database - the exact scenario
        store_write_safe exists to guard against), the row was NOT
        actually removed, yet $still_queued was still reported as
        false/0 - so cli/retry-download.pl and cli/retry-transcription.pl
        printed an unqualified RETRY OK and exited 0, even though the row
        remained in failed_downloads/failed_transcriptions.
      - Both functions now capture store_write_safe's own success flag
        for the removal write and derive $still_queued from it, instead
        of hardcoding 0. This is a previously-unaddressed sibling of the
        exact bug class TGT-244/TGT-247/TGT-248 already fixed for the
        record_message-failure path; cli/retry-download.pl and
        cli/retry-transcription.pl needed no changes - they already
        consumed the 3rd return value correctly, only the value they were
        given was wrong in this one edge case.

1.99  2026-09-14
      - BUGFIX (TGT-248, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly bug hunt):
        the exact TGT-247 bug class, unfixed in
        D2TG::Download::retry_failed_transcription - the function TGT-247's
        own Changes/docs entries name as retry_failed_download's
        documented structural sibling. It always returned
        (1, $transcript) once download+transcription succeeded,
        regardless of whether the follow-up record_message write then
        succeeded. When record_message failed (e.g. a transient
        locked/busy database), the row was correctly left queued in
        failed_transcriptions (never removed) and no row was ever
        written into the messages table - yet cli/retry-transcription.pl
        still printed the ordinary "RETRY OK [id] ...: <transcript>"
        line with exit 0, even though d2 tg.history/d2 tg.unread would
        never show the recovered message.
      - retry_failed_transcription now returns a 3rd value,
        $still_queued (mirroring retry_failed_download's own
        TGT-244/TGT-247 signal), true exactly when record_message
        failed. cli/retry-transcription.pl now branches on it: prints
        "RETRY PARTIAL" instead of "RETRY OK", explains the history
        record could not be written yet, names the row as still queued
        for automatic or manual retry, and sets a non-zero exit code -
        instead of falsely claiming full success. Added
        t/248-retry-transcription-record-failure-partial.t (a real unit
        test against D2TG::Download::retry_failed_transcription using a
        D2TG::Store subclass that forces record_message to die, matching
        t/196/t/245's own established pattern for this module).

