1.96  2026-09-14
      - BUGFIX (TGT-245, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly bug hunt):
        D2TG::Download::retry_failed_download and
        retry_failed_transcription both called $store->record_message
        without a bot_key argument, even though the queue row they were
        retrying (failed_downloads/failed_transcriptions, TGT-219/
        TGT-237) already carried its own bot_key. TGT-232 migrated the
        messages table to a (chat_id, message_id, bot_key) composite
        key and threaded bot_key through every record_message call
        site it enumerated, but never enumerated these two retry
        functions. In a multi-bot config, a message that failed
        download/transcription under a non-default bot and was later
        successfully retried (manually via d2 tg.retry-download/
        d2 tg.retry-transcription, or automatically via TGT-221's
        auto_retry_failed_downloads) had its restored history recorded
        under D2TG::Store::DEFAULT_BOT_KEY instead of the bot that
        actually received it - invisible to
        'd2 tg.history --bot <token>'/'d2 tg.unread --bot <token>' for
        that bot, and able to silently ON CONFLICT-overwrite an
        unrelated default-bot message sharing the same
        (chat_id, message_id). Both record_message calls now pass
        bot_key => $row->{bot_key}, matching every sibling call site.
        New regression coverage: t/245-retry-record-message-bot-key.t.

1.95  2026-09-14
      - BUGFIX (TGT-244, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly bug hunt):
        D2TG::Download::auto_retry_failed_downloads' own 60s auto-retry
        throttle (TGT-221) was silently bypassed for a queued
        failed_downloads row whose file downloaded successfully but
        whose record_message bookkeeping write kept failing - exactly
        the "persistently-failing record_message" scenario TGT-196
        already documented as real. retry_failed_download always
        returned (1, $local_path) once the download itself succeeded,
        even though the row was deliberately left queued
        (mark_failed_download_downloaded) when record_message failed;
        auto_retry_failed_downloads only stamped last_retry_at when
        that return was false, so a row stuck in this exact
        partial-success state never got last_retry_at stamped and was
        retried on every single poll cycle instead of once per
        AUTO_RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS (60s). retry_failed_download now
        returns a third value, $still_queued, so auto_retry_failed_downloads
        can stamp last_retry_at whenever the row remains queued after
        an attempt, regardless of which step (download or
        record_message) caused it to stay queued. New test:
        t/244-auto-retry-throttle-record-message-failure.t.

