1.57  2026-09-11
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-194, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly
        bug hunt, reproduced live in a developer-dashboard:latest
        container, the same class of issue as TGT-132/165/166/186/
        190/191/192/193): D2TG::Download::retry_failed_download called
        $store->record_message(...) and $store->remove_failed_download(...)
        directly, with no eval/classification around either call -
        the one unwrapped D2TG::Store write pair in this module. If
        either died (e.g. a locked/busy database), the
        exception propagated raw straight out of retry_failed_download,
        breaking its own documented (1, $local_path)/(0, $error)
        return contract even though the download itself genuinely
        succeeded. cli/retry-download.pl's own batch mode has no eval
        around this call either, so the exception crashed the whole
        script mid-loop, silently abandoning every remaining queued
        row in that batch. Both calls now eval-wrapped and classified
        via D2TG::Poller::_classify_store_error, matching the
        established _store_write_safe/_record_message_safe/
        persist_offset_safe pattern - the reported (1, $local_path)
        success is unaffected by a bookkeeping-write failure, since
        the download itself did succeed. remove_failed_download is
        deliberately only attempted when record_message either
        succeeded or wasn't needed - a record_message failure never
        removes the queue row (a Codex documentation-stage review
        finding: doing so unconditionally would leave a message with
        neither a queue row nor a history record, worse than the
        pre-fix crash, which at least left the row queued). A
        repo-wide sweep for this bug class, done as part of that same
        review, found one more remaining unwrapped call pair -
        cli/approve.pl's own approve/is_allowed calls - filed
        separately as TGT-195, not yet fixed. New test
        t/194-retry-download-store-write-non-fatal.t (19 assertions),
        confirmed genuinely red against the pre-fix code (the whole
        test script crashed with an uncaught die, no TAP plan
        produced).

1.56  2026-09-11
      - CONSISTENCY FIX (TGT-193, found via a scheduled JOB-004
        improvement hunt): D2TG::Poller::run_once's 4 STORE ERROR print
        blocks (is_allowed x3 - message_reaction, edited_message, plain
        message branches; add_pending x1 - plain message branch) each
        printed the raw DBI/SQLite exception text verbatim to STDERR,
        instead of classifying it via the already-established shared
        _classify_store_error helper - every other D2TG::Store error
        path IN THIS MODULE already does this (is_allowed is a read,
        not a write, but is still a D2TG::Store call whose failure this
        pattern classifies)
        (_record_message_safe TGT-132/133, persist_offset_safe
        TGT-166/191). A Codex QA-stage review finding: this claim is
        scoped to D2TG::Poller.pm specifically, not the whole codebase -
        D2TG::Reply's own _store_write_safe (TGT-192) is a separate
        module with its own equivalent pattern, and
        D2TG::Download::retry_failed_download has the identical
        unwrapped-call gap, tracked separately as TGT-194, not yet
        fixed. These 4 call sites predate _classify_store_error (TGT-165, before
        TGT-167 extracted the shared helper) and were simply never
        revisited. A raw DBI/SQLite error can embed the database
        file's own real path - the same disclosure risk TGT-133
        established as this project's standard to avoid. New test
        t/193-store-error-classified-not-raw.t (12 assertions,
        covering all 4 call sites), confirmed genuinely red against
        the pre-fix code (8/12 assertions failed, each showing the raw
        db_path/exception text leaking into STDERR).

1.55  2026-09-11
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-192, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly
        bug hunt, the same class of issue TGT-191 just fixed):
        D2TG::Reply::send_reply/resend_voice's own record_sent_text/
        record_sent_voice/mark_read calls were the one D2TG::Store
        write call site in this codebase never wrapped in eval - every
        sibling already classifies a failure via
        D2TG::Poller::_classify_store_error rather than letting the
        raw exception (which can embed the real db_path) propagate.
        record_sent_text runs against a RaiseError=>1 handle, so a
        locked/busy database died raw AFTER send_message had already
        succeeded - aborting the rest of send_reply entirely (skipping
        voice synthesis, which runs unconditionally afterward) and
        reporting a hard failure that could even suggest retrying,
        risking a duplicate text delivery. All 5 call sites now go
        through a shared _store_write_safe helper - eval-wrapped,
        classified, logged non-fatally. Since the die no longer aborts
        the sub, voice synthesis/send naturally still runs afterward,
        and a store-write failure specifically can no longer turn an
        otherwise-successful send_reply call into a reported hard
        failure - synthesis/send_voice themselves still fail loudly
        exactly as before (TGT-083's own tradeoff unchanged). New test
        t/192-store-write-failures-non-fatal.t (23 assertions,
        covering both send_reply and resend_voice), confirmed
        genuinely red against the pre-fix code (the whole test script
        crashed with an uncaught die).

        A second Codex QA-stage finding on this same fix: the initial
        version evaluated $voice_result->{message_id} INSIDE the
        _store_write_safe closure, risking a malformed (non-hashref)
        $voice_result being misclassified as a non-fatal
        "record_sent_voice failed" STORE ERROR rather than propagating
        as the more serious failure it actually is. Moved the
        extraction outside _store_write_safe's own eval.

        A third round on the same finding, after the first attempt at
        moving it turned out to still be wrong: $voice_result->{message_id}
        was wrapped in its own eval and the store write was simply
        skipped when that eval caught something - but dereferencing a
        hash key off undef in Perl's rvalue context does not raise an
        exception at all (eval { undef->{key} } leaves $@ empty), so
        that eval could never actually distinguish a malformed
        (non-hashref) result from a well-formed hashref that's simply
        missing the key - both silently produced undef, and the
        malformed case was now reported as a plain, silent success
        instead of ever propagating as a failure (worse than the round
        2 misclassification, not better). Fixed by checking
        ref($voice_result) eq 'HASH' explicitly in both send_reply and
        resend_voice: a non-hashref result now dies for real, but only
        when a store was actually given and the text send produced a
        usable message id (the store write's own pre-existing gating -
        a caller that never passes store, or whose send_message result
        carried no usable id, is unaffected either way, same as before
        this ticket) - matching TGT-083's "voice failures are loud,
        never silent" tradeoff, and the pre-TGT-192 behavior for the
        cases that genuinely did die back then. A present-but-incomplete
        hashref (e.g.
        Fake::ReplyTelegram's own 'shapeless' option) still quietly
        skips just the store write, exactly as before. New regression
        blocks cover both shapes independently.

        A fourth round: the docs overclaimed the non-hashref die as
        unconditional (it's gated on store+text_message_id being
        present, matching the store write's own pre-existing gating -
        qualified in README/Changes/POLICIES/commands.md), and the
        malformed/shapeless coverage and the "left unread" guarantee
        had no resend_voice-specific assertions - added both.

        A fifth round found that "left unread" guarantee was actually
        FALSE for resend_voice: its own mark_read call ran BEFORE the
        ref() eq 'HASH' check, so a malformed voice result died only
        after the message had already been marked read, defeating the
        retry/recovery state resend_voice exists to preserve. Fixed by
        reordering resend_voice so mark_read only runs after the
        result shape is confirmed usable. Confirmed red against the
        pre-fix ordering first.

        A sixth round found the reordered check was still gated on
        store+text_message_id, while mark_read is gated on the
        strictly broader store+reply_to_message_id - so a caller
        giving store and reply_to_message_id but not text_message_id
        could still slip a malformed voice result past the check
        entirely and have it marked read anyway (present identically
        in both send_reply and resend_voice). Fixed by checking the
        result shape whenever store is given at all, independent of
        text_message_id. New tests prove the die still fires with
        text_message_id omitted, for both functions.

1.54  2026-09-11
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-191, live production incident via the
        budget project: 2 real Telegram messages permanently lost):
        cli/poller.pl's main loop advanced its in-memory poll offset
        unconditionally after each cycle, regardless of whether
        D2TG::Poller::persist_offset_safe actually durably saved it.
        Telegram's own getUpdates offset parameter is a confirmation
        mechanism, not just a cursor - it forgets/never redelivers an
        update once a LATER offset is sent. A still-running process
        would use the advanced-but-unpersisted offset on its own next
        getUpdates call, confirming that batch to Telegram; a crash
        for any reason before a later persist caught up then lost that
        batch forever - structurally identical to the live incident.
        persist_offset_safe now returns a true/false success flag
        (previously always void); the main loop only advances the
        in-memory offset on a true return, so a failed persist reuses
        the same offset next cycle and Telegram redelivers the batch
        instead of discarding it (deduplicated locally via
        record_message's own upsert and run_once's own already-
        recorded check, TGT-178). New test
        t/191-offset-not-advanced-on-persist-failure.t simulates a
        persist-fails-then-succeeds scenario, confirmed genuinely red
        against the pre-fix unconditional-advance behavior.

1.53  2026-09-11
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-190, filed from TGT-187's own
        investigation): D2TG::Config::changes_summary silently
        returned undef with zero diagnostic whenever no entry could be
        matched for the requested version against an otherwise-
        readable Changes file - not only a genuine wrong-version
        mismatch, but also a header line whose version matches but
        whose own shape is malformed. Now prints a non-fatal STDERR
        diagnostic naming the requested version and, if the file has
        one, a recognizable header found elsewhere in it - or says
        explicitly that none was found, if it doesn't (two Codex
        QA-stage review rounds: the diagnostic searches for the first
        strictly-shaped header anywhere in the file, so it can skip a
        malformed earlier one; and a file with no strictly-shaped
        header at all names none, rather than always naming one)
        before returning undef unchanged - matching this project's
        established
        non-fatal-degradation pattern (skill_version_check_safe/
        persist_offset_safe). A genuinely missing/unreadable Changes
        file still returns undef silently, with no diagnostic - only a
        readable file with no matching entry is covered.

