      - BUG FIX (TGT-185, filed from a Codex QA-stage review on
        TGT-184): cli/poller.pl's D2TG::Store->new failure exit
        (TGT-183) ran before releasing the startup lock file
        D2TG::Lock::acquire had already acquired above it, leaking a
        stale telegram.pid lock on that failure. Fixed by releasing
        the lock before that exit, matching the same fix TGT-184
        already applied to heartbeat_path's own identical gap.
        Investigated the ticket's second originally-scoped scenario
        (the "no --chat_id/--bot groups configured" exit) and found
        it unreachable dead code - two earlier startup guards already
        refuse before that check can ever see an empty groups list -
        documented in the new test rather than faking a scenario that
        cannot actually occur.
      - BUG FIX (TGT-185, second Codex QA-stage review round): found
        two MORE exit paths after D2TG::Lock::acquire leaking the
        lock - a "no bot tokens configured" exit, and the
        exec()-restart-failure die. Rather than patching a fourth
        site individually, added a single END block right after
        D2TG::Lock::acquire that releases the lock on every
        Perl-managed exit/uncaught die past that point - standard Perl
        END-block behavior, it does not run if the process terminates
        on an untrapped signal (SIGKILL always, or any signal without
        an installed handler at that moment); no claim made about
        exactly when SIGTERM/SIGINT become safe. D2TG::Lock's own
        staleness/eviction logic recovers a lock left behind that way
        on the next poller start, not automatically - a structural fix
        for the whole bug class. Idempotent and
        PID-scoped, safe
        alongside the existing explicit release() calls.

