1.44  2026-09-10
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-179, found via a scheduled JOB-003 hourly
        bug hunt, reproduced live via a stalled FIFO): D2TG::Transcribe
        ::_probe_duration's ffprobe call had no timeout at all, unlike
        every sibling subprocess call in this codebase - it runs
        synchronously in transcribe() before the retry loop's own
        timeout scoping even begins, so a hang here blocked the ENTIRE
        single-threaded poller indefinitely for every chat. Now runs
        via D2TG::Subprocess::fork_in_own_process_group (a new
        optional stdout-capture param) under a waitpid(WNOHANG) poll
        loop bounded by $PROBE_TIMEOUT (10s), killing the process
        group on timeout, rather than a plain alarm()-around-a-
        blocking-readline (which does not reliably interrupt a
        buffered pipe read - PerlIO retries on EINTR). A timed-out
        probe falls back to 0 duration exactly like every other probe
        failure mode already did.

1.43  2026-09-10
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-178, implementing Michael's own Q-011
        design ruling on TGT-176's message-loss investigation):
        run_once's returned offset used to advance past an update
        whose local record_message write failed regardless, and
        Telegram never redelivers an update once the offset has moved
        past it - a genuine store write failure (locked/busy/readonly
        database) permanently and silently lost that message's local
        history. _record_message_safe now returns a success/failure
        flag; run_once caps the returned offset at the first failing
        update's own update_id encountered while iterating the batch
        in order instead of the batch's full offset, so Telegram redelivers it (and everything after it in
        the same batch) next cycle. record_message's own existing
        PRIMARY KEY(chat_id, message_id) upsert already makes a
        redelivered message's store write idempotent - no schema
        change needed. To avoid re-announcing an already-recorded
        update on redelivery, run_once now checks store->get_message
        before acting on a plain message and skips it when already
        found.

1.42  2026-09-10
      - INVESTIGATION (TGT-176, no code change this entry - tracked
        under 1.42 since it's the same incident window): identified a
        real, confirmed design-level gap (a local record_message write
        failure lets Telegram's offset advance past an update that was
        never actually persisted, so it can never be redelivered) as
        the most plausible - but not conclusively confirmed as the
        actual - cause of the reported incident; evidence instead
        points more toward extended downtime from TGT-175's own
        (then-unfixed) crash bug as the likelier primary cause.
        Michael's own architectural ruling: stop advancing the offset
        past a failed write, let Telegram redeliver, dedupe the
        resulting duplicate. Implemented as TGT-178 (see 1.43 above).
        Full write-up in docs/POLICIES.md.
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-175, live production incident reported via
        the budget project, Michael's own owner chat): cli/poller.pl's
        main-loop version-change check called
        D2TG::Config::skill_version() directly, unwrapped - a transient
        window where .env was briefly missing/unreadable during the
        skill's own self-update (an install rewriting the directory
        mid-flight) was fatal to the ENTIRE poller process, killing the
        owner's own Telegram channel for about a minute until a human
        noticed monitor-dead and manually restarted the job. New
        D2TG::Poller::skill_version_check_safe wraps the call, matching
        persist_offset_safe's (TGT-166) own non-fatal-degradation
        pattern - a failure is logged to STDERR and the version-change
        check is skipped for that cycle only, retried next cycle. The
        poller's own startup call to skill_version (which still refuses
        to start loudly on a genuinely missing .env at launch) is
        deliberately unchanged - only this periodic re-check, made once
        the process is already running, is safe to degrade.

