1.30  2026-09-10
      - SECURITY HARDENING (TGT-162, found via scheduled hourly bug hunt
        JOB-003, second pass): D2TG::Telegram::_send_file's caption
        field (from cli/send.pl's --caption flag) was spliced into the
        raw hand-built multipart body with zero sanitization, unlike
        the adjacent filename field, which was hardened for TGT-125
        (control-character stripping plus quote/backslash escaping)
        after a Codex review found the original escaping alone
        insufficient against header injection. The same trust boundary
        (a user-supplied cli/send.pl argument) got defense-in-depth on
        one field and none on the other. Caption sits in body content
        rather than a quoted header attribute, so its own real risk is
        narrower: if the caption text happens to embed the literal
        per-call multipart boundary string ('D2TGBoundary' .
        int(rand(1e9)) . time, regenerated and not attacker-visible
        each call) in real delimiter syntax (\r\n--$boundary with valid
        trailing framing, not the bare value alone), it could
        prematurely terminate the multipart body, letting trailing
        bytes be reinterpreted as new form fields (e.g. clobbering
        chat_id in the same request). _send_file now conservatively
        strips any occurrence of the literal boundary string from the
        caption - delimiter-shaped or not - before inserting it into
        the body. Deliberately does
        not add filename's own quote/backslash escaping or control-
        character stripping to caption - those exist to prevent header
        injection, which does not apply to plain body content where
        CR/LF is legitimate caption text. send_voice's own multipart
        construction is untouched (already reviewed, different code
        path).

