0.70  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-094, live production incident found during TGT-093's
        own install): the poller's version-change self-restart (TGT-036)
        used to exec() the literal $0 path captured at launch. TGT-093
        renamed cli/poller to cli/poller.pl while a real poller was
        mid-poll; its version check fired and the exec died against the
        now-nonexistent old path, killing the poller (~1 minute real
        outage, recovered manually). Fixed: new
        D2TG::Config::resolve_self_exec_path re-checks the script's own
        bin directory for the current poller.pl basename at restart
        time, falling back to $0 only if that lookup fails - resilient
        to a rename because only the filename changed, not the
        directory.

0.71  2026-09-08
      - DOC ACCURACY (TGT-095, found via JOB-005's scheduled doc-accuracy
        hunt): docs/commands.md's cli/*.pl entrypoint summary line listed
        only 5 of the 6 real files - cli/help.pl (d2 tg.help, TGT-089)
        was missing, even though it's documented elsewhere in the same
        file. Corrected.

0.72  2026-09-08
      - IMPROVEMENT (TGT-096, live user request via Telegram): d2
        tg.reply's send_reply call was completely unwrapped in
        cli/reply.pl, so any failure propagated as a raw Perl fatal
        with no guidance for the calling agent. New
        D2TG::Reply::format_send_error appends an explicit retry
        instruction when the error looks transient (a network timeout
        or a 5xx status), matching D2TG::Telegram's own die message
        shapes - a permanent failure (bad token, invalid chat_id) is
        unaffected, with no misleading retry suggestion.

0.73  2026-09-08
      - IMPROVEMENT (TGT-097, live user request via Telegram): d2
        tg.poller no longer prints a POLL ERROR line for a
        known-transient poll failure (a network timeout or a 5xx
        status) - it keeps retrying silently instead, since these are
        routine and self-heal via the existing retry loop with no
        operator action needed; each occurrence was previously reaching
        the project's tira.policy.bridge as its own separate
        monitor-output event, pure noise. A genuinely unexpected/
        non-transient poll failure is still reported as before. New
        D2TG::Config::is_transient_error is a shared classifier now
        used by both this and D2TG::Reply::format_send_error (TGT-096),
        avoiding duplicated regex (same precedent as TGT-072).

0.74  2026-09-08
      - IMPROVEMENT (TGT-100, live user request via Telegram): a voice
        note transcribed with the fixed default 'medium' Whisper model
        could exceed D2TG::Transcribe's $TIMEOUT (300s) and get killed,
        losing the transcript entirely instead of degrading gracefully.
        New D2TG::Transcribe::select_model($duration_seconds) tiers the
        model by audio length - <=5min stays 'medium' (unchanged
        quality for the common case), <=15min drops to 'small', longer
        uses 'base' - and transcribe() now probes duration via ffprobe
        (list-form pipe open, no shell involved) before invoking
        whisper. An explicitly-passed model still overrides the
        automatic choice, unchanged from before.
      - FOLLOW-UP (TGT-100, same live user request, after Michael's
        measured data): duration-based tiering alone was insufficient -
        per-host Whisper throughput varies (medium measured at ~5.6x
        real time on his host, no GPU; a 102.48s clip took 9m31s real
        time, well inside the original 300s "stays on medium"
        threshold). transcribe() now automatically retries at the next
        faster model tier (medium -> small -> base) whenever the
        current automatically-selected model times out, only dying with
        a clear TRANSCRIBE ERROR if even 'base' times out. An
        explicitly-passed model is never automatically retried -
        explicit still means explicit.
      - FOLLOW-UP (TGT-100, same live user request, msg #137/#138):
        D2TG::Poller::run_once now prints a "NEW TG VOICE [chat_id]
        sender: transcribing... (this may take a few minutes)" notice
        immediately, before the (potentially multi-minute, blocking)
        transcription call, instead of the watched stream staying
        silent until the real transcript line arrives afterward.

0.75  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-098, found via a scheduled hourly bug-hunt
        investigating cross-module interactions between multi-bot
        support and access control): D2TG::Store's allow_list/pending
        tables were keyed only by chat_id, no bot dimension. A Telegram
        GROUP chat's chat_id is shared across every bot that's a member
        of it (unlike a private chat's id, which Telegram allocates
        uniquely per bot) - so approving a group for one of this
        skill's configured bots silently also approved it for every
        other bot sharing the same store. Both tables now carry a
        composite (chat_id, bot_key) PRIMARY KEY, migrated in place
        from any pre-existing single-column-PK database (SQLite can't
        ALTER a PRIMARY KEY, so a pre-migration table is rebuilt:
        renamed aside, replaced, every row copied across with
        bot_key='' - the single-bot/unscoped sentinel, deliberately not
        SQL NULL since SQLite's uniqueness checks don't treat two NULLs
        as equal). is_allowed/add_pending/approve/_seed_admin all gain
        an optional bot_key parameter; cli/approve gains an optional
        --bot <token> flag (matching cli/reply's own, TGT-057) that
        defaults to the single-bot sentinel when omitted - unchanged
        behavior for every existing single-bot install.

0.76  2026-09-08
      - REFACTOR (TGT-101, found via a scheduled improvement-hunt review
        of the TGT-098 migration code): D2TG::Store's bot_key='' sentinel
        was repeated as a bare literal in 8 places (2 CREATE TABLE DDL
        strings, the migration block's own CREATE TABLE/INSERT strings,
        and 4 "unless defined" guards) with no single source of truth.
        Now a single `use constant DEFAULT_BOT_KEY => ''`,
        matching this project's existing use-constant convention
        (D2TG::Poller's TELEGRAM_GETFILE_MAX_BYTES, D2TG::Lock's
        constants, D2TG::Telegram's DEFAULT_HARD_TIMEOUT). Pure refactor,
        no behavior change - full 612-test suite passes unchanged.

0.77  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-102, found via a scheduled hourly bug-hunt (JOB-003)
        investigating D2TG::Lock.pm fresh): if an existing lock names
        the caller's own PID - exactly what cli/poller.pl's
        version-triggered self-restart produces every time, since
        exec() preserves the PID - acquire() now succeeds immediately
        without unlinking or recreating the lock file. Previously,
        own-PID re-entry reached the fallback reclaim path (intended
        for a dead/unreadable PID) and briefly churned the lock file,
        opening a window where a competing poller could apply the
        normal live-conflict takeover policy (TGT-084) against the
        legitimately self-restarting process. Reproduced live in a
        developer-dashboard:latest-equivalent Docker container; may
        also explain some of this session's earlier unexplained
        "poller monitor job died silently" incidents. TGT-084's
        existing reclaim/live-conflict-kill behavior for a genuinely
        different PID is unchanged (t/52/54/66/67 all green).

0.78  2026-09-08
      - BUGFIX (TGT-107, live-experienced incident: user-supplied
        feedback reported cli/poller.pl silently accepting `--help` as
        an ordinary, ignored argument and starting a real, long-running
        poll loop - which, per D2TG::Lock's own "last one wins"
        (TGT-084), SIGKILLed the legitimate poller already holding the
        lock, taking the bot offline): cli/poller.pl now validates its
        argv fully - `--help`/`-h` prints a short usage summary and
        exits 0; any other unrecognized argument refuses with a clear
        error naming it, exit 1 - before the storage location/lock are
        ever touched. A real ordering bug surfaced during implementation
        (moving bot_groups() earlier initially broke the D2TG_CHAT_ID-
        missing guard's own message) was caught by the full suite and
        fixed before shipping. Every previously-recognized flag
        (--db/-d/--chat_id/--bot) is unchanged.

