0.97  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Telegram::_send_file (TGT-125, found via a scheduled
        hourly bug-hunt): the local file's basename is now escaped
        before insertion into the multipart Content-Disposition
        header's filename="..." attribute (backing d2 tg.send's
        outbound photo/document uploads). A literal double-quote
        previously prematurely closed the quoted attribute, corrupting
        the header and producing a malformed multipart request sent to
        Telegram's Bot API. Backslashes are escaped first, then quotes,
        matching the standard MIME quoted-string escaping convention
        (RFC 7578, the current multipart/form-data spec).
      - A Codex review found the quote/backslash escaping alone was
        insufficient: a filename can legally contain a literal CR/LF on
        Unix, which would inject an additional raw header line into the
        multipart request regardless of the quoting fix. Now strips C0
        control characters and DEL entirely first - the file still
        sends correctly with a sanitized displayed filename, rather
        than refusing the whole send.

0.98  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Download::download_file (TGT-126, found via an ad-hoc
        bug-hunt): the HTTP GET that fetches inbound photo/document/
        voice file bytes had no explicit LWP timeout and no SIGALRM-
        based hard-timeout wrapper - the same failure class already
        fixed once in D2TG::Telegram (TGT-035, TGT-044), where LWP's
        own 180s default proved unreliable for a request stuck in the
        initial TCP connect() phase and could wedge the poller
        indefinitely, immune even to SIGTERM. download_file's
        LWP::UserAgent now defaults to an explicit timeout, and the
        get() call itself is wrapped in a new _with_hard_timeout
        helper (matching D2TG::Telegram::_with_hard_timeout's own
        SIGALRM pattern) so a hung download dies with a clear timeout
        message instead of hanging the poll cycle forever.

0.99  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::TTS::_run (TGT-127, found via an ad-hoc bug-hunt
        immediately after TGT-126): the gtts-cli/ffmpeg external
        commands were invoked via a bare system(@cmd) with no timeout
        at all - unlike every other outbound call in this codebase
        (D2TG::Telegram, D2TG::Download), which now has a SIGALRM-
        based hard timeout. gtts-cli specifically makes a real network
        call to Google's TTS endpoint, and every outbound reply
        (D2TG::Reply::send_reply, resend_voice) runs synthesis
        synchronously, so a single hung gtts-cli/ffmpeg call could
        wedge the entire reply path indefinitely. _run now forks and
        execs the command itself (in its own process group), waits
        under a SIGALRM hard timeout, and kills the whole process
        group if it hangs, dying with a clear timeout message instead
        of blocking forever. synthesize()'s two call sites also now
        clean up their temp files on a thrown die, not just a non-zero
        exit status.

1.00  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Transcribe::_run (TGT-128, found via an ad-hoc bug-hunt
        immediately after TGT-127): whisper's subprocess had no
        process-group protection - on a timeout, only the immediate
        whisper process was signalled, leaving any child processes
        whisper itself spawned (whisper CLIs commonly shell out to
        ffmpeg/ffprobe-family tooling for audio decoding, and this same
        module's own _probe_duration already depends on ffprobe)
        running/orphaned. _run now puts the child in its own process
        group (both parent and child call setpgrp immediately after
        fork, closing a race the sibling D2TG::TTS fix's own review
        caught) and signals the whole process group - plus the direct
        pid as a fallback - on both the TERM and KILL timeout steps.

1.01  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Store::new (TGT-129, found via an ad-hoc bug-hunt): sets
        PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000 and PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL on
        every connection. DBD::SQLite's default busy timeout is 0, so a
        concurrent writer (the long-running d2 tg.poller process writes
        on every inbound message while other independently-invoked d2
        tg.* commands - reply, approve, retry-download - also write
        against the same db_path) previously got an immediate
        "database is locked" error instead of a brief, usually-
        successful wait - the exact SQLite concurrency robustness
        pattern this project's own research notes on the original
        Python blueprint had flagged as worth keeping, but which had
        been dropped in the Perl port.

1.02  2026-09-09
      - SKILLS.md's cli/*.pl file list (TGT-130, found via an ad-hoc
        bug-hunt): was missing cli/tts.pl, which TGT-106 shipped after
        TGT-123's own fix to this exact list had already landed - the
        same doc-drift class recurring. Added, and a new regression
        test (t/98-skills-md-cli-list-current.t) now checks this list
        against the real cli/*.pl file list on every run, so a future
        drift fails the suite instead of accumulating silently again.

1.03  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Transcribe::kill_current (TGT-131, found via an ad-hoc
        bug-hunt immediately after TGT-128): only signalled the direct
        tracked pid, inconsistent with _run's own timeout-path process-
        group protection TGT-128 had just established for the exact
        same running process. cli/poller.pl's SIGTERM/SIGINT shutdown
        handlers call kill_current directly, so a clean poller shutdown
        mid-transcription could still leave a whisper-spawned child
        process running even though a timeout-triggered kill already
        reached it. kill_current now signals the whole process group
        plus the direct pid, matching _run's own pattern exactly.

1.04  2026-09-09
      - Never expose a downloaded attachment's real local filesystem
        path anywhere agent-facing (TGT-133, live request via
        Telegram): D2TG::Poller's NEW TG MEDIA line used to print the
        real path directly, and D2TG::Store persisted that same path
        into the message summary text shown verbatim by
        d2 tg.history/d2 tg.unread. Matches this project's own Tira
        board convention (tira.attachment.get writes raw content to
        stdout, never a path). New d2 tg.attachment <chat_id>
        <message_id> command writes a stored attachment's raw bytes to
        stdout; the poller now prints "GET ATTACHMENT WITH: d2
        tg.attachment <chat_id> <message_id>" instead of a path, one
        instruction per attachment. D2TG::Store gained a separate
        local_path column (never surfaced by any display path) and a
        narrow get_attachment_path accessor; D2TG::Download's retry
        path was updated to match the same convention.

1.05  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Poller::run_once (TGT-132, found via an ad-hoc bug-hunt):
        all 4 record_message calls (text, transcribed voice, downloaded
        media, and the no-callback fallback branch) are now eval-wrapped
        via a new _record_message_safe helper, matching the sibling
        record_failed_download call's own established pattern (TGT-104).
        Previously, a D2TG::Store write failure mid-batch (e.g. SQLite
        contention exceeding TGT-129's own busy_timeout) would die
        uncaught inside run_once; run_once_safe catches that by
        returning the poll offset UNCHANGED, causing the entire batch -
        including updates already printed and handled - to be
        redelivered and reprinted next cycle, risking a duplicate reply
        from the watching agent. A record_message failure is now logged
        non-fatally to stderr instead, and the rest of the batch (and
        the offset advance) proceeds normally. A Codex review caught
        that the raw exception text was initially printed verbatim - a
        DBI/SQLite error can embed the database file's own path, an
        information-disclosure surface this project just spent TGT-133
        closing off elsewhere - now classified into a short, fixed
        reason instead of ever echoing the exception itself.

1.06  2026-09-09
      - cli/attachment.pl (TGT-134, self-review immediately after
        TGT-133 shipped): d2 tg.attachment's refusal for a missing
        stored file now names D2TG::Download::prune_vault's own
        byte-cap eviction as the likely cause, rather than the same
        generic "cannot open" message every other open failure got.
        local_path (TGT-133) never expires from the database, but the
        file it names can - an old, never-re-fetched attachment
        eventually ages out once the vault fills past its 100MB
        default cap. Also now rejects a recorded path that exists but
        isn't a regular file with its own distinct message. Documented
        explicitly in docs/commands.md and docs/POLICIES.md: fetching
        an attachment is only reliable for one still within the
        vault's currently-retained set, not a permanent guarantee.

1.07  2026-09-09
      - SKILLS.md (TGT-135, self-review while completing TGT-134's
        pending-push gate): section 1 ("What this skill is"), the
        onboarding overview a new reader sees first, now names
        d2 tg.attachment and the never-expose-a-real-path guarantee
        explicitly - previously only section 6's later step-by-step
        walkthrough mentioned it, so a reader skimming just the
        overview had an incomplete picture of how downloaded media
        actually works.

1.08  2026-09-09
      - docs/commands.md (TGT-136, self-review while completing
        TGT-135's pending-push gate): added a worked example to the
        d2 tg.attachment section showing the full three-line stdout
        sequence a photo/document message actually produces (NEW TG
        MEDIA, GET ATTACHMENT WITH, REPLY WITH), and noting that
        multiple media messages in one poll cycle each get their own
        independent GET ATTACHMENT WITH line. Previously this flow was
        only described in separate prose across two sections.

1.09  2026-09-09
      - D2TG::Config::masked_token (TGT-138, self-review while
        completing TGT-136's pending-push gate): the fallback for a
        token too short to usefully mask (<8 characters) used to return
        the token unmodified - the exact opposite of what a masking
        function exists to prevent. Now returns a fixed placeholder,
        "(short token, not shown)", instead. Real Telegram bot tokens
        are always far longer than 8 characters, so this path was never
        reachable in normal operation, but a masking function's one
        edge case must never be the one that leaks the full secret.
        This edge case was previously identified and deliberately
        scoped out of TGT-115 (whoami) as that ticket's own concern -
        this ticket picks up that deferred work.
